BlacKkKlansman is a 2018 American biographical crime comedy-drama film directed by Spike Lee and written by Charlie Wachtel,
David Rabinowitz, Kevin Willmott and Lee, loosely based on the 2014 memoir Black Klansman by Ron Stallworth.

12
00:54:24,000 --> 00:54:30,000
The film stars John David Washington as Stallworth, along with
Adam Driver, Laura Harrier, and Topher Grace.

13
00:58:56,000 --> 00:59:02,000
It also features Harry Belafonte's last
performance before his death in April 2023.

14
01:03:28,000 --> 01:03:34,000
Set in the 1970s in Colorado Springs, Colorado, it follows the first African-American detective in the
city's police department as he sets out to infiltrate and expose the local Ku Klux Klan chapter.

15
01:08:00,000 --> 01:08:06,000
The film was produced by Lee, Raymond Mansfield, Shaun Redick,
Sean McKittrick, Jason Blum, and Jordan Peele.

16
01:12:32,000 --> 01:12:38,000
It was packaged by Andy Frances, Stallworth's manager.

17
01:17:04,000 --> 01:17:10,000
QC Entertainment purchased the film rights to the book in 2015.

18
01:21:36,000 --> 01:21:42,000
Lee signed on as director in September 2017.

19
01:26:08,000 --> 01:26:14,000
Much of the cast joined the following month, and
filming began in New York State.

20
01:30:40,000 --> 01:30:46,000
BlacKkKlansman premiered on May 14, 2018, at the Cannes
Film Festival, where it won the Grand Prix.

21
01:35:12,000 --> 01:35:18,000
It was theatrically released in the United States on August 10, 2018, a day before the
first anniversary of the Unite the Right rally in Charlottesville, Virginia.

22
01:39:44,000 --> 01:39:50,000
The film received critical acclaim, with praise for Lee's direction, the
performances (particularly of Washington and Driver), and timely themes.

23
01:44:16,000 --> 01:44:22,000
Critics  noted it as a return to form for Lee.

24
01:48:48,000 --> 01:48:54,000
It received six nominations at the 91st Academy Awards, including Best Picture, Best
Director (Lee's first directing nomination), and Best Supporting Actor for Driver.

25
01:53:20,000 --> 01:53:26,000
It won for Best Adapted Screenplay, Lee's first competitive Academy Award.

26
01:57:52,000 --> 01:57:58,000
The American Film Institute also selected it as one of the top 10 films of 2018.

27
02:02:24,000 --> 02:02:30,000
At the 76th Golden Globe Awards, it earned four
nominations, including Best Motion Picture – Drama.

28
